Atomicity: per the Phase A audit, tick-manufacturing/tick-extraction were
the least atomic paths in the codebase (a non-locking availability read,
followed later by a separate loop of unlocked per-row `.update()`/`.delete()`
calls — a real TOCTOU window no other economic path in this codebase has).
Two new `service_role`-only RPCs close this, following the same pattern as
`charge_employee_wage_atomic` (098) and the `settle_*_atomic` family:
`run_extraction_slot_production` and `run_manufacturing_line_production`.
Each does input relief (via the Phase C shared
`compute_inventory_cost_relief` helper, under `SELECT ... FOR UPDATE`) and
output creation (via the existing `add_business_inventory_quantity`, called
as a nested function so its weighted-average addition logic isn't
duplicated) in one transaction. A shortfall raises
`insufficient_input:<item_key>` instead of returning a false/failure value,
specifically so every input already relieved earlier in that same call
(other recipe ingredients, e.g.) rolls back too — a plain early return would
have let already-executed UPDATE/DELETE statements commit. TS catches that
specific message and treats it exactly like the pre-existing "not enough
inputs" skip (no console.error, no job/slot treated as failed) rather than
letting it fall into the generic per-job/per-slot try/catch that logs real
failures.

Both extraction and manufacturing decouple "input consumed this tick" from
"output produced this tick" (independent floor()/remainder progress
accumulators per 053) — a tick can relieve input cost without yet producing
a unit. Added `pending_production_cost` (numeric(14,2)) to both
`extraction_slots` and `manufacturing_lines` as a small WIP/suspense
account: relieved input cost accumulates there across ticks and is fully
absorbed into the next produced batch's cost basis, then reset to 0, so
cost is never lost or double-counted when consumption and production don't
line up 1:1 in a single tick.

tick-manufacturing keeps its existing non-locking pre-check loop
(`canProduce`/`inventoryConsumptionPlan`) as a cheap early-exit — it still
decides whether to even attempt the RPC, and still supplies the
quality-weighted-average calculation (`resolveManufacturingQuality`), which
stays a TS/gameplay concern, not an accounting one. What changed is that the
pre-check no longer performs or trusts the actual mutation — the RPC
re-validates atomically under row locks regardless, so the pre-check
answering "yes" is now just an optimization, not the authority. Its
`unitCost` field/`resolveRowUnitCost` call was deleted since the RPC computes
cost from each row's own total_cost/unit_cost internally.

