   Balance Sheet / Income Statement / Cash Flow Statement, per items 47-49.
   Deliberately sources revenue/COGS/payroll/fixed-assets from
   `business_financial_events` (grouped by `account_code`, the same Chart of
   Accounts vocabulary migration 108's journal lines use) rather than
   literally requiring `business_journal_lines` coverage for every account:
   every revenue-generating path in this codebase already writes correct,
   complete `business_financial_events` rows as of Phases B-F (confirmed by
   each phase's own audit), while the literal double-entry journal
   deliberately has the buy-order gap documented above. Using
   financial_events as the statements' source means every path is
   represented; the journal remains available as an independent
   reconciliation source for the paths that do post to it (that
   cross-check is Phase H's reconciliation-RPC job, item 53, not
   re-implemented here).
   - Balance Sheet: Cash via `get_business_account_balance`; Inventory =
     `SUM(business_inventory.total_cost)` (never market price, per item
     47/50's explicit rule); Fixed Assets net of straight-line depreciation
     (see below); Wages Payable = `SUM(employees.unpaid_wage_due)` (the
     running liability balance Phase B already maintains -- simpler and
     equally authoritative than re-deriving it from the journal, which is
     payroll-only anyway); Owner Capital / Draw from `business_accounts`
     categories (`opening_capital`+`owner_transfer_in` / `owner_transfer_out`);
     Retained Earnings = cumulative net income to date (calls
     `getIncomeStatement` with `from: null`). Returns a `balanced` boolean
     (`Assets == Liabilities + Equity` within a cent) rather than asserting
     it internally -- a real, reportable check per item 55, not a
     guaranteed invariant (see the in-transit-shipment gap above).
   - Income Statement: Revenue/COGS/Payroll Expense/Operating Expense summed
     from `business_financial_events` by `account_code` for the requested
     period; Depreciation Expense computed analytically (not journal-posted)
     as the delta in accumulated depreciation between period start and end.
     Operating Income = Gross Profit - all operating expenses; Net Income =
     Operating Income (no other income/expense modeled).
   - Cash Flow Statement: every `business_accounts` row in the period,
     classified Operating/Investing/Financing via a new
     `CASH_FLOW_CLASSIFICATION` map (`src/config/finance.ts`), signed by its
     own `entry_type`. Requires `beginningCash + netCashFlow == endingCash`
     (returned as a `reconciles` boolean) by construction -- all three
     figures derive from the same rows. `CASH_FLOW_CLASSIFICATION` is
     deliberately separate from the pre-existing
     `BUSINESS_LEDGER_CATEGORY_CLASSIFICATION` (used by finance.ts's legacy
     accrual-style dashboard): `shipping_fee` is an Operating cash outflow
     per item 49's own worked example even though it's capitalized into
     inventory, not expensed, on the accrual Income Statement (item 45) --
     the two classifications intentionally diverge for that one category.
   - Straight-line depreciation: new `FIXED_ASSET_USEFUL_LIFE_MONTHS = 60`
     config constant (`src/config/finance.ts`) -- one global default per
     item 46's "simple configurable useful life," not a per-upgrade field
     (business-upgrades.ts's ~100 entries have no such field, and adding one
     is a bigger change than this plan calls for). `computeAccumulatedDepreciation`
     treats a month as exactly 30 days (a documented simplification, not
     calendar-accurate) and is computed analytically at report time from
     each `fixed_assets` financial event's amount + `effective_at` -- there
     is no periodic depreciation tick or journal posting; Phase F's own
     notes already flagged this as deliberately deferred to Phase G.

   Fixed the item-47/50 violation this phase's own audit found in the
